  "image_name": "2c222d5f1674cf45b2db2b34b1c635fc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the HIV life cycle and highlights where major antiretroviral drugs and broadly neutralizing antibodies act. It shows an enveloped virus attaching to a host cell through CD4 and the co-receptor CCR5, then entering the cell. Early steps are blocked by attachment inhibitors and CCR5 antagonists. After entry, the viral RNA is reverse-transcribed into DNA, a step targeted by several reverse transcriptase drug classes, including nucleoside, nucleotide/translocation, and non-nucleoside inhibitors.\n\nThe viral DNA then moves into the nucleus and integrates into the host genome, where integrase strand transfer inhibitors act. New viral material is produced, translated into Gag and Gag-Pol proteins, and assembled at the cell membrane. The figure then shows virus budding and a final maturation step that converts immature particles into infectious virions; protease inhibitors block this process, leaving particles structurally immature.\n\nAn inset emphasizes that broadly neutralizing antibodies recognize key exposed sites on the viral envelope spike, including the CD4 binding site, V1–V2 and V3 loop regions, the gp120–gp41 interface, and MPER. Overall, the image links HIV replication stages with the principal therapeutic intervention points.",
